On 17 Oct 2006 08:43:15 -0700, defklown wrote:

>> The only times i see "stamina" in the nethack source code is for cases
>> when you're burdened:
>
>Now I'm gonna pose the obvious followup question. How does one avoid
>being burdened, especially when playing a class that starts off with
>low strength? 

  Focus on what's important:  
   - Armor
   - weapons.  
   - One, maybe two known escape items.  
     - Let me emphasize KNOWN escape items.  
       - Carrying unidentified items on the off-chance they'll help 
         doesn't work in the long run.  If nothing else, you'll run out.
       - Work on staying out of trouble instead.
   - Pet treats to keep your pet close.

  Stash other items by the downstairs just before you descend.  That
keeps harmful items from being used against you.  It also keeps useful
items from being used up.

  The first box or chest you find is a godsend for your stash.

>(I think strength is the only contributing attribute but
>I could be wrong) 

  Strength and Con.

>Obviously, not picking things up is the way to go,
>but then you screw yourself over due to a lack of items.

  Carrying too many items doesn't work that well.  You'll forget to
examine your inventory and then not use them.

  Instead, focus on a few tricks, learn them well, and execute them to
perfection:
  - Elbereth early and often
  - Courageously flee!
  - Hide behind pets
  - ranged attacks
    - throw rocks if nothing else
  - Try on every non-cursed piece of armor you find for AC improvement.

> I guess, to be
>specific, what are some good ways to safekeep items without carrying
>them? 

  As above.  Chest, box, or even a sack are great.  If you have a plain
or oilskin sack you can drop it by the downstairs as you start to
explore a level.

>Or how does one determine what items are necessary, or at least
>what items are more important, when increasing one's load? 

  AC and doing damage, especially damage at range.  Daggers are the best
thrown weapon for every class.  Pick up and throw darts as well, they're
light enough to be worth it.

>Generally, what are some strategies to minimize load weight?

  When you hit "Burdened" stash stuff.  I don't care how much it hurts.
Some of your early decisions will be wrong.  That's NetHack.

>
>I don't know if it's just me, but the carrying capacity in Nethack is
>incredibly difficult to cope with unless playing as a barbarian or
>something. And even then I have a tough time with it.

  That's called being a Packrat.  Breaking that habit is one of the most
important early tricks.


